/**
 * @page rbc_calling_conventions Shared C argument and lifetime conventions
 *
 * @section rbc_interp_argument Interpreter arguments
 * A Tcl_Interp pointer named interp is normally borrowed for the duration of
 * the call. It identifies the interpreter used for results, diagnostics and
 * interpreter-owned objects; passing it does not transfer ownership. Do not
 * assume NULL is accepted unless the function says so. TCL_ERROR normally
 * leaves a diagnostic, but legacy helpers with different behavior document it.
 * Preservation keeps a record allocated; it does not prevent interpreter
 * deletion or make interpreter services valid after deletion.
 *
 * @section rbc_command_arguments Tcl command arguments
 * In a registered object command, objc counts borrowed objv entries and objv[0]
 * names the command. Treat the array as read-only. RBC dispatchers may pass a
 * shifted argument vector to a private handler; that handler's contract defines
 * its positions and arity. Do not retain an entry beyond its guaranteed lifetime
 * without taking a Tcl reference, or mutate shared Tcl objects without following
 * Tcl copy-on-write rules. Conversions that evaluate expressions can run Tcl.
 *
 * @section rbc_client_data Callback client data
 * ClientData is an untyped pointer, not an ownership annotation. Each callback
 * must specify its concrete target (for example a graph, vector, or stack-local
 * status flag), who owns it, and when it remains valid. Registration does not
 * itself imply a reference count or preservation guarantee.
 *
 * @section rbc_output_arguments Results and output pointers
 * An output pointer is not necessarily an ownership transfer. Each function
 * describes whether a returned object is borrowed or owned, which release
 * operation applies, and whether outputs remain unchanged, are cleared, or may
 * be partly initialized on failure. A Tcl status alone does not promise rollback.
 *
 * @section rbc_callback_lifetimes Reentry and object lifetime
 * Run interpreter-owned operations in the owning thread. Tcl evaluation,
 * variable traces, notifications and custom storage destructors can invoke
 * client code. Preserving a record does not preserve its old buffers or prevent
 * renaming, remapping, locking or destruction. Revalidate the specific state
 * needed after a callback; documented local checks are not a universal guarantee.
 */
